theory.intro:
An algebraic expression is built from **terms**, separated by addition. The number multiplying a pronumeral in a term is its **coefficient**, and a term that is just a number is the **constant term**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.4]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\node (a) at (0,0) {$a$};
\node (x) at (0.32,0) {$x$};
\node (m) at (0.95,0) {$-$};
\node (c) at (1.5,0) {$c$};
\draw (-0.25,0.4) -- (-0.25,0.58) -- (0.6,0.58) -- (0.6,0.4);
\node at (0.175,0.92) {term};
\draw (0.72,0.4) -- (0.72,0.58) -- (1.78,0.58) -- (1.78,0.4);
\draw (1.25,0.58) -- (1.25,1.25);
\node at (1.25,1.5) {constant term};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.35) -- (0,-0.32);
\node at (0,-1.7) {coefficient of $x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
